  "result": "**Why it matters:** Your assistant must check the booking calendar. The model cannot do that alone, because it only writes text.\n\n**The idea:** Picture a person in a closed room. They can only pass notes through a slot. They cannot open the door. A helper stands outside.\n\nNext to the slot is a menu: \"check calendar\", \"book machine\", \"search manual\". Each line says what the job does.\n\n**Example:** A student asks, \"Is the laser cutter free on Friday?\"\n1. The person writes a note: \"check calendar: laser cutter, Friday.\"\n2. The helper does the job outside the room.\n3. The helper slides back the result: \"Free 2–4 pm.\"\n4. The person reads it and writes the answer to the student.\n\n**Key point:** The model never runs a tool. It only writes a request. Your code runs the tool and returns the result. The model only knows the menu you gave it. So a clear tool description matters a lot.\n\n**Where the analogy breaks:** The helper here is your Python code. It can also refuse a bad request.\n\nWant a short lesson on this, with more examples from the makerspace assistant?\n\nQuick check: in the story, who actually looks at the calendar?",
